        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Does NSF/ANSI Certification Mean on a Water Filter Pitcher?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It means an accredited third party has confirmed that the cartridge reduces specific, named contaminants, and the proof sits in the <strong>performance data sheet</strong>, not on the box. Every pitcher claims to make water cleaner, but only a few can show it for contaminants that affect health. Brands must publish the data sheet for certified claims, and it lists each contaminant, the influent challenge level used and the percentage reduction achieved. If a brand makes that sheet hard to find, treat its marketing as unverified.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The four standards printed on pitcher boxes</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>NSF/ANSI 42</strong> covers aesthetic effects such as chlorine taste and odor and particulates, and says nothing about health. <strong>NSF/ANSI 53</strong> covers health-related contaminants, including lead, mercury, cysts and certain volatile organic chemicals, plus PFOA and PFOS under newer listings. <strong>NSF/ANSI 401</strong> covers emerging compounds such as some pharmaceuticals, pesticides and BPA. <strong>NSF/ANSI 473</strong> is the standard dedicated to PFOA and PFOS.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Certification is granted per contaminant</h3>
          <p>
            A certification applies to individual contaminants, not to the product as a whole. A cartridge can be certified to Standard 53 for cysts alone and still print the Standard 53 number on the box, which is how a pitcher marked NSF 53 on the front can do nothing for lead. Before trusting the claim, find the word lead, or PFOA and PFOS, next to a reduction percentage on the data sheet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who did the certifying matters</h3>
          <p>
            The certifier counts too. NSF International, IAPMO, the Water Quality Association and CSA are all accredited, and a listing from any of them means a third party evaluated the product and audits the factory. Wording such as <strong>tested to NSF standards</strong> is weaker: it can mean the brand paid a lab to run the protocol once, with no ongoing audits. That has some value, but it does not equal a certification you can look up in a public listing.
          </p>

