        <div id="what-to-remove" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Pitcher Filter Do You Need for Your Water?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Let your water report decide: a lead-certified cartridge for older plumbing, a PFOA and PFOS claim for PFAS, and a basic Standard 42 cartridge if taste is the only issue. On a public system, your utility mails or posts an annual <strong>Consumer Confidence Report</strong> listing detected contaminants and their levels; search your utility's name plus CCR. If you rent or live in an older house, that report will not show lead from your own service line or plumbing, because lead usually enters at the house, not at the treatment plant.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Older homes and lead</h3>
          <p>
            Houses built before 1986 can have lead solder on copper pipes, and many built before 1950 still have lead service lines. In that case a certified lead filter is among the cheapest health purchases you can make, and the right choice is a cartridge certified to Standard 53 for lead at the 150 parts per billion challenge level. Many local health departments and utilities offer free or low-cost lead testing kits, which give you a baseline before and after you start filtering.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">PFAS and taste-only problems</h3>
          <p>
            For PFAS, look for Standard 53 or 473 claims that name PFOA and PFOS. The EPA finalized enforceable drinking water limits for several PFAS compounds in 2024, and many utilities are still working toward compliance, so a pitcher can serve as a reasonable bridge. If the complaint is only taste, chlorine smell or a little sediment, a basic Standard 42 carbon cartridge fixes it for the least money and with the fastest pour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Private wells</h3>
          <p>
            Private wells raise different risks. Bacteria, nitrates and arsenic are the main concerns, and most pitchers carry no certification for bacteria or nitrate at all. A pitcher does not replace testing a well at least once a year through a state-certified lab. If those results show a problem, the usual fix is a point-of-entry system or a reverse osmosis unit, both covered in detail in our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">home water filtration system guide</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Taste problem: any Standard 42 cartridge. Lead or PFAS: Standard 53 or 473 for that specific contaminant.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy the certification your water report calls for, then choose among the pitchers that carry it on size, speed and cost. Paying for the priciest pitcher without reading the report wastes money about as often as it helps.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

