        <div id="filter-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Is the Difference Between Carbon, Ion Exchange and Membrane Pitcher Filters?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carbon handles taste and many chemicals, ion exchange handles metals and dissolved minerals, and a membrane physically blocks particles and microbes; most cartridges combine at least two of them. Knowing which one a cartridge relies on explains most of its strengths and its running cost.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Activated carbon</h3>
          <p>
            Nearly every pitcher cartridge begins with <strong>activated carbon</strong>, whose enormous internal surface area captures chlorine, many organic chemicals and the compounds that make water taste off. Granular carbon, the loose black beads in older cartridges, works quickly but leaves gaps water can slip through. Pressed or bonded carbon blocks push water through a tighter matrix for better contact, which is why the stronger lead and PFAS cartridges use them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ion exchange resin</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Ion exchange resin</strong> trades unwanted ions, such as lead, copper and the minerals behind hard water, for harmless ones. Most mixed cartridges pair resin with carbon to cover both metals and taste. ZeroWater goes further with a five-stage design that strips out nearly all dissolved solids, which is why its included meter reads close to zero on a fresh filter. It also removes calcium and magnesium, so some people find the water flat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Membrane microfilters</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Membrane microfilters</strong>, like the one in LifeStraw pitchers, physically stop particles above a stated pore size. They target bacteria, parasites and microplastics, which matters more for well or travel water than for treated city supply. A membrane clogs gradually rather than failing all at once, and flow slows as it loads. It works alongside a carbon stage, not in place of one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why no pitcher uses reverse osmosis</h3>
          <p>
            No pitcher uses reverse osmosis, because RO needs household water pressure to drive water through its membrane and a gravity pitcher has almost none. If you need RO-level reduction of fluoride, nitrate, arsenic or total dissolved solids, a countertop or under-sink RO unit is the realistic next step. Cartridges that claim to rival RO usually score well on a handful of contaminants, not across the board.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="daily-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Fast Does a Water Filter Pitcher Filter, and Will It Fit Your Fridge?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A basic carbon cartridge typically fills the reservoir in two to five minutes, while a dense block certified for lead and PFAS can take 10 to 15 minutes per refill. Flow speed is the complaint owners raise most, and it trades directly against filtration depth; owners commonly report that dense blocks slow further toward the end of a cartridge. If nobody in the house will wait, everyone drifts back to the tap, which defeats the purchase.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Real capacity versus the cup count</h3>
          <p>
            Cup counts mislead because they include the unfiltered top reservoir. A 10-cup pitcher usually gives about 5 to 6 cups of filtered water per fill, which is fine for one or two people. A family will either refill constantly or do better with a dispenser: an 18- to 27-cup tank with a spigot that sits on a fridge shelf, where slow flow matters less because you refill it only once or twice a day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Measure the fridge door and check the lid</h3>
          <p>
            Measure your fridge door before you buy. Most door shelves take pitchers about 5 inches deep and up to 11 inches tall, and many larger models will not fit. A pitcher banished to a main shelf gets pushed to the back and forgotten. Check the lid too: owners commonly report that a lid that pops off mid-pour is the most irritating daily flaw, ahead of flow speed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Filter-as-you-pour designs</h3>
          <p>
            Some households are better served by a filter-as-you-pour design such as Brita Stream, which cleans water as it passes through the spout so you can pour as soon as you fill it. The catch is that this format is typically certified for chlorine taste and odor rather than lead, so it suits taste-only problems best. Faucet-mount filters make the same compromise: faster, but bulky on the tap.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filter-changes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When Should You Change a Water Filter Pitcher Cartridge?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Change it when it reaches its rated gallons or its time limit, whichever comes first, and sooner if flow slows sharply or the water turns cloudy. Rated life is the volume over which the cartridge was certified to keep performing; beyond it there is no evidence it still reduces lead or PFAS, even if the water tastes fine. Taste is a poor guide, because chlorine reduction usually outlasts the health-related media, so treat the gallon rating as a hard limit rather than a suggestion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How accurate are lid indicators?</h3>
          <p>
            Most pitchers come with a lid indicator. Basic versions are timers counting days since you pressed a button, accurate only if your use matches the brand's assumption of about 20 gallons a month. Better indicators count lid openings, which follows real use more closely. If you drink more than average, set a phone reminder based on gallons rather than months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Prepare every new cartridge</h3>
          <p>
            Nearly every cartridge needs a soak or flush before first use. Carbon fines, harmless black specks, are normal in the first pitcher or two, so pour away the first one or two fills as the instructions say. Pre-soak any cartridge that floats or runs slowly from new, since trapped air is the usual cause, and push it in until it clicks to prevent bypass, where water flows around the filter instead of through it.
          </p>

