        <div id="pitcher-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Can't a Water Filter Pitcher Do?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pitcher cannot treat water you do not drink, cannot disinfect, and does little for fluoride, nitrate or dissolved solids. It handles only drinking and cooking water, so it does nothing for shower water, laundry, dishwasher spotting or the scale building up in a <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">water heater</a>, and it will not clear the hair and soap behind a slow bathroom sink, which is a job for a <a href="/best-drain-snake/">drain snake</a>. Hard water, rust stains or sulfur smell throughout the house need a point-of-entry fix: a whole-house filter, a softener or an iron filter where the main line enters, which is a different budget and a different purchase.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pitchers do not disinfect</h3>
          <p>
            Unless a particular cartridge carries a certified microbiological claim, assume it does not remove bacteria or viruses, and never use a pitcher in place of a boil-water notice. Filtered water also loses the residual chlorine that kept it sanitary in the pipes, so keep the pitcher in the fridge and finish the contents within a day or two.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fluoride, nitrate and dissolved solids</h3>
          <p>
            Most pitchers barely touch fluoride, nitrate or total dissolved solids. ZeroWater is the main exception for dissolved solids, and a few specialty cartridges claim fluoride reduction, but none matches an RO system. If your report shows nitrate above the federal limit of 10 milligrams per liter, particularly with an infant in the house, use bottled water or a certified RO system until it is fixed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When you outgrow a pitcher</h3>
          <p>
            Volume is the other ceiling. Once a household drinks more than about two gallons a day, refilling becomes a chore and cartridge spending climbs. At that stage an under-sink filter with its own faucet is usually cheaper per gallon and far more convenient. Compare the options in our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">best water filtration system for home</a> rankings before buying another multipack.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Do You Clean a Water Filter Pitcher?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hand-wash the reservoir, lid and body with mild dish soap at every cartridge change, and ideally every couple of weeks in between. A filter pitcher is a damp container holding water with no chlorine in it, so it can develop a slimy film or a green tint if neglected. Rinse thoroughly so no soap reaches the new filter. Most plastic bodies are top-rack dishwasher safe, but check the manual, since high heat can warp lids.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep it cold and out of the sun</h3>
          <p>
            Store the pitcher away from direct sunlight, which encourages algae, and away from the stove, ideally in the fridge. Never run hot water through it: carbon cartridges are rated for cold water, and heat can damage the media and release what it captured. For tea or coffee, filter cold water first and boil it afterward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">After a vacation</h3>
          <p>
            If you are away for more than a week, pour out the water in the pitcher and leave the cartridge in place. Some brands suggest running one fresh fill through before drinking again. A cartridge that has dried out completely should be soaked again as if it were new; owners commonly report that a dried cartridge pours slowly until it is fully re-wetted.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recycle old cartridges</h3>
          <p>
            Recycle spent cartridges where you can. Brita runs a free mail-back program through TerraCycle, and ZeroWater has offered a credit for returned cartridges. Programs change, so check each brand's website. Several cartridges a year is little waste next to bottled water, but a free recycling route is still worth using.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes With Water Filter Pitchers
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the front of the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Phrases like reduces up to 99 percent or NSF certified on the front of the box never say which contaminants are covered. Certification is granted per contaminant, so a pitcher can hold one for chlorine taste and nothing that affects health. Open the performance data sheet online and find lead or PFOA/PFOS by name first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running cartridges past their rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water can taste perfectly fine long after a cartridge has stopped reducing lead, because chlorine removal is the last thing to go. The gallon rating is your only evidence that the health claims still apply. If your household drinks more than about 20 gallons a month, track gallons rather than months.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filtering hot water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pitcher cartridges are designed and certified for cold water only, and hot water can damage the carbon and release trapped contaminants back into the pitcher. Filter cold, refrigerate, then heat the water for tea, coffee or cooking. Faucet-mount filters follow the same rule, and running a pitcher under the hot tap to speed it up is never a shortcut.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the first flush</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new cartridge sheds harmless carbon dust and can hold trapped air, which shows up as black specks, a floating filter and painfully slow flow. Soak or flush it as directed and pour away the first one or two fills. Then seat it until it clicks so water cannot sneak around the media.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a pitcher for well water without testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bacteria, nitrate and arsenic are the main private-well risks, and most pitchers are certified for none of them. Have the well checked by a state-certified lab before buying anything. If the results show a problem, a pitcher is the wrong tool; a certified point-of-use or point-of-entry system is the right one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

