        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which water filter pitcher removes lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One whose cartridge is certified to NSF/ANSI 53 for lead specifically, with that claim on its performance data sheet. Brita's Elite filter, PUR Plus, ZeroWater and Clearly Filtered all list certified lead reduction; Brita's standard white filter and the Stream filter do not. Check the current data sheet, because certifications can change between cartridge generations and sometimes between pitcher bodies.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do water filter pitchers remove PFAS?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some do. Look on the data sheet for PFOA and PFOS listed under NSF/ANSI 53 or 473. Clearly Filtered lists broad PFAS reduction, and a few other cartridges carry PFOA/PFOS claims. Do not rely on a basic carbon cartridge certified only to Standard 42 for PFAS. Where PFAS levels are high, an under-sink reverse osmosis system gives a wider margin.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I change a pitcher filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the rated gallon capacity or the time limit, whichever arrives first. Standard cartridges are usually rated for 40 gallons or about two months, and long-life cartridges for 100 to 120 gallons. A family drinking two gallons a day reaches 40 gallons in under three weeks, so track volume instead of trusting the calendar indicator alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my water filter pitcher filter so slowly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                New cartridges often trap air, so soak them as the instructions describe. Dense carbon blocks certified for lead and PFAS are slow by design, often taking several minutes per refill, and flow also falls as a cartridge fills with sediment near the end of its life. If a new filter stays very slow after soaking, reseat it firmly or exchange it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is filtered pitcher water safe to leave out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Filtering removes the residual chlorine that kept the water sanitary in the pipes, so bacteria can grow faster at room temperature. Keep the pitcher in the refrigerator, drink the filtered water within a day or two, and wash the pitcher with mild soap every few weeks. Keep it out of direct sunlight, which encourages algae.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is ZeroWater better than Brita?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That depends on your water. ZeroWater removes nearly all dissolved solids and is certified for lead and chromium, so it suits soft or moderate water and people who want a neutral taste. On hard water its cartridges run out quickly and cost far more per gallon. The Brita Elite filter is cheaper to run, certified for lead and rated for longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a pitcher or an under-sink filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pitcher fits renters, small households and anyone drinking less than about a gallon or two a day. An under-sink filter costs more up front and needs a simple install, but it flows faster, often costs less per gallon over time and can include reverse osmosis. If you refill a pitcher several times a day, the sink system usually pays off.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
